  "comparison": {
    "neural_vs_majority_delta": 0.5101214574898785,
    "neural_vs_risk_level_mapped_delta": 0.6336032388663968,
    "beats_both_baselines": true,
    "diagnosis": "Primary model (18 features incl. the literal rule-defining booleans) reaches heldout accuracy=1.0000, beating majority-class baseline (0.4899) by +0.5101 and risk_level-mapped baseline (0.3664) by +0.6336. Because the label rule is itself a deterministic OR/threshold function of these same signals, near-perfect accuracy here mainly shows the small MLP CAN learn that nonlinear OR-of-conditions / weak-evidence-count-threshold boundary from raw component signals via gradient descent, instead of a hand-coded lookup table -- it is not, by itself, evidence of novel generalization beyond the rule's own inputs. The antishortcut ablation model (rule-defining booleans removed, 7 continuous/ordinal features only) reaches heldout accuracy=0.9494, still clearly beating both deterministic baselines from softer continuous signals alone (stale_hours, review_wait_hours, risk_level ordinal, risks/notes counts) -- this is the more meaningful generalization signal for future neural-bridge migration."
